// ValueTupleReturn — THE TWO-REGISTER RETURN CONVENTION FOR A SMALL VALUE TUPLE.
//
// A function returning a two-element tuple hands its pair back as a heap record: the callee
// `__mm_alloc`s two slots, writes both words, returns the pointer, and the caller reads the two words back
// out and drops the box. This pass finds the shapes where that record is pure ceremony and puts the two
// words in the two RETURN REGISTERS instead, deleting the allocation at both ends.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS A THIRD DISJUNCT OF AN ABI THAT ALREADY EXISTS, NOT A NEW ONE.** shv2 already returns a
// second machine word for a THROWING function (the error flag) and for an INTERFACE-RETURNING one (the
// witness half of the fat pointer) — `functionUsesSecondaryReturnSlot`. `StdOp.errorReturn` is that
// dual-register epilogue and `StdOp.tryCall` is the call that captures both halves, on every target. So
// there is no new Std op and no new backend arm here: the pass rewrites `ret` into `errorReturn` and
// `call` into `tryCall`, and stamps `IrFunction.usesValueTupleReturn` so the wasm signature builder
// declares the second result the body now writes.
//
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// ⭐⭐⭐ THE GATE: A VALUE-CONVENTION FUNCTION HOLDS NO RECORD AT ALL, WHICH IS WHY OWNERSHIP NEVER MOVES
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// The obvious wider rule — *"any non-throwing function returning a two-slot tuple"* — is a DOUBLE FREE
// waiting for a managed element. Under the heap convention a returned record OWNS its two elements and
// ownership travels to the caller with the pointer; under a register convention the caller receives two
// bare words. Reading the halves out of a record the callee did not mint — a borrowed parameter, a record
// that also escaped into an array — and handing them over as owned would release them twice, and
// retaining them per element is not something this tier can express: a tuple over a TYPE PARAMETER, which
// is what `stdlib`'s `MapIterator.current()` returns, has no statically known element drop at all.
//
// ⇒ **So the rule is that a value-convention function's return NEVER TOUCHES A RECORD.** Every `ret` in it
// must be one of exactly two shapes (plus the unreachable panic return a range guard appends, which reads
// no record because it returns a constant — see `TupleValueRow.isAnUnreachableZero`):
//
//   • **A FRESH LITERAL** — the returned value is an `__mm_alloc` made in this very function whose only
//     uses are its two half stores and that `ret`. Nothing else can see the box, so deleting it and
//     returning the two STORED VALUES moves exactly the ownership the record was carrying, whatever the
//     elements are. That is `pair`'s shape, and `MapIterator.current()`'s.
//   • **A FORWARDED PAIR** — the returned value is the result of a call to another value-convention
//     function and its only use is that `ret`. The pair arrives in registers and leaves in registers, and
//     no record is built in between.
//
// A `return t` of a borrowed PARAMETER, and a `return p` of a record that also escaped, are neither — so
// those functions keep the heap convention entirely, and every element-ownership question with them.
//
// ⭐ **AND THE CALLER MUST BE ABLE TO TAKE THE PAIR TOO, OR THE CALLEE DOES NOT GET THE CONVENTION.** A
// call site qualifies when the result's only uses are whole-slot loads of its two halves plus the one
// trivial `__mm_decref` that drops it — no record needed, so none is built — or when the result flows
// straight into the enclosing function's own value-convention `ret`. Anything else (the pair escapes into
// an array, its drop is a `__destruct_<T>` cascade because the elements are managed at THIS instantiation,
// it crosses a branch merge) takes the callee OFF the convention for the whole compile. That is what keeps
// this pass from ever having to materialize a record out of two registers — which in a `--debugstream`
// build would also have to invent an mm-trace tag for a box no source line asked for.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH ENDS READ ONE VERDICT, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SAFETY ARGUMENT.** A callee lowered to
// `errorReturn` whose caller still reads one register hands back a scalar where a pointer is expected; the
// reverse drops a word. So the decision is made ONCE, module-wide, and both rewrites are driven from it.
//
// ⛔ **THE VERDICT IS A FIXPOINT, because the two conditions are mutually recursive** — a `ret` may forward
// another candidate's pair, and a call site may forward into its own caller's `ret` — so a refused
// candidate is removed and the removal propagates until nothing changes. The loop is bounded by the
// candidate count (functions returning a two-element tuple at all) and re-reads only the two verdict
// lists; the per-function analysis behind them is done once, before it.
//
// ⚠ **ONLY A TUPLE.** A named struct with two word fields has the same layout and is deliberately
// excluded: a struct has reference identity a user can observe (`is`, aliasing, mutation through an
// alias), and while the call-site test above refuses every site that observes it, widening this pass to
// user records is a different rung with a different acceptance.
//
// Scheduled last in `PassPipeline.buildLoweringPasses`, after `promoteStackRecords`, so every shape it
// matches is the final one — a record a promotion already turned into frame storage is no longer a call
// this pass can see, and the two therefore cannot both claim one site.

// A tally of one value's uses, by category. Counts, not indices: the rewrite walk re-reads the ops it is
// changing, so nothing here has to remember where they were.
typealias UseTally = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias UseTallyArray = Array with UseTally

// Op indices this pass deletes. A `Set` and not a dense column: op indices are MODULE-wide, so a dense
// mark would cost one bool per op in the whole program for each function rewritten, and the doomed set is
// a handful of entries. (`StdDialect.removeOpsDefining` deletes by DEF instead, which cannot serve here:
// a `storeIndirect` into a deleted record defines nothing.)
typealias DoomedOpSet = Set with OpIndex

// A tuple that rides the two return registers is exactly two whole machine words, at the offsets
// `LayoutDescriptor` gives a record of two word-sized fields.
let LowHalfOffset = 0 as ByteOffset
let HighHalfOffset = MachineWordBytes

// `__mm_alloc(size, destructor)` — plus a trace tag in a `--debugstream` build, which this pass reads
// past: it deletes the allocation, so the tag loses its reader with it.
let MmAllocMinArgCount = 2 as ParamCount
let MmDecrefArgCount = 1 as ParamCount

// A call that is not the one-argument `__mm_decref` a record's whole drop lowers to.
enum NotTheTrivialDrop
	someOtherCall
end 'NotTheTrivialDrop'

// The value a trivial drop releases. THE shape this pass admits as a record's release: a
// `__destruct_<T>` cascade — which is what the drop lowers to when the elements are managed at this
// instantiation — is some other call and refuses the pair. Asked by the census, by the classifier's
// complement and by the rewrite, so the three cannot come to disagree about what a trivial drop is.
function trivialDropOf(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray) returns ValueId throws NotTheTrivialDrop
	if not callee.equals(MmDecrefName) or args.count() != MmDecrefArgCount 'notTheTrivialDrop'
		throw NotTheTrivialDrop.someOtherCall
	end 'notTheTrivialDrop'
	return try args.get(0) otherwise panic("trivialDropOf: args.get(0) OOB on a one-argument `__mm_decref`")
end 'trivialDropOf'

// Whether a dense mark column answers true for `value`. The columns are minted over one function's value
// space, and a value minted by the rewrite itself lies past their extent — outside is a `false` answer,
// not an error.
function columnMarks(column BoolArray, value ValueId) returns bool
	if value >= column.count() 'outsideTheValueSpace'
		return false
	end 'outsideTheValueSpace'
	return try column.get(value) otherwise panic("columnMarks: column.get OOB at {value} after a bounds test")
end 'columnMarks'

// How a value this pass reasons about is DEFINED, which decides which rewrite it can take.
union TupleValueOrigin
	// `__mm_alloc(...)` in this function: a record that may be elidable at its `ret`.
	freshBox
	// A direct `call` to a candidate: a pair that may arrive in registers.
	directCall(calleeIndex FuncIndex)
	// A `const` in this function. A tuple-returning function cannot return a constant, so the only way one
	// reaches a `ret` here is `InsertRangeChecks.emitDeadReturn` — the unreachable terminator it appends
	// after `mrt_panic` so a guard block still LEAVES the function the way the ABI says.
	constant
	// A parameter, a block argument, a load — anything else. Neither rewrite applies, and saying so by
	// NAME is what makes a returned parameter a refusal rather than an absence.
	elsewhere
end 'TupleValueOrigin'

// One value's census within one function: where it comes from, and what reads it.
//
// ⚠ **`storedLow`/`storedHigh` MEAN SOMETHING ONLY WHERE THE MATCHING TALLY IS 1**, exactly as
// `typeNameIdOf`'s name column means something only on the arms whose tag carries a name. They are read
// under that guard and nowhere else.
type TupleValueRow
	export var value as ValueId
	export var origin as TupleValueOrigin
	export var loadLow as UseTally
	export var loadHigh as UseTally
	export var storeLow as UseTally
	export var storeHigh as UseTally
	export var returnUses as UseTally
	export var trivialDrops as UseTally
	// Every read this pass has no rewrite for: an argument to some other call, a store of the value
	// ITSELF into somewhere, a branch-edge argument, a comparison. One is enough to refuse.
	export var other as UseTally
	export var storedLow as ValueId
	export var storedHigh as ValueId

	export static function create(value ValueId) returns TupleValueRow
		return Self{value: value, origin: TupleValueOrigin.elsewhere, loadLow: 0, loadHigh: 0, storeLow: 0, storeHigh: 0, returnUses: 0, trivialDrops: 0, other: 0, storedLow: 0, storedHigh: 0}
	end 'create'

	// The FRESH-LITERAL shape: this box is written once at each half and returned, and nothing else in the
	// function can see it. Both stores must be there or the `errorReturn` would have no second word.
	export function isAnUnsharedFreshPair() returns bool
		return self.storeLow == 1 and self.storeHigh == 1 and self.returnUses == 1 and self.loadLow == 0 and self.loadHigh == 0 and self.trivialDrops == 0 and self.other == 0
	end 'isAnUnsharedFreshPair'

	// The CALL-SITE shape that needs no record: the halves are read out and the box is released by the one
	// trivial decref. Zero loads is admitted — a result nobody reads still costs the call.
	export function isReadThenDropped() returns bool
		return self.trivialDrops == 1 and self.returnUses == 0 and self.storeLow == 0 and self.storeHigh == 0 and self.other == 0
	end 'isReadThenDropped'

	// ⛔⛔ **THE UNREACHABLE PANIC RETURN, which is not an exception to the gate but the same rule applied to
	// a block that never runs.** A range-guarded parameter gives its function one `ret <zero const>` per
	// guard (`InsertRangeChecks.emitDeadReturn`), appended AFTER lowering and therefore against the
	// convention this pass has not yet decided. Nothing reads either register there — `mrt_panic` does not
	// return — but the block must still leave the function with the arity its type declares, or the wasm
	// module does not validate, which is the X6 defect that door's own header records.
	export function isAnUnreachableZero() returns bool
		return self.returnUses >= 1 and self.loadLow == 0 and self.loadHigh == 0 and self.storeLow == 0 and self.storeHigh == 0 and self.trivialDrops == 0 and self.other == 0
	end 'isAnUnreachableZero'

	// The FORWARDED shape: the pair's one use is the enclosing function's own return.
	export function isForwardedToTheReturn() returns bool
		return self.returnUses == 1 and self.trivialDrops == 0 and self.loadLow == 0 and self.loadHigh == 0 and self.storeLow == 0 and self.storeHigh == 0 and self.other == 0
	end 'isForwardedToTheReturn'
end 'TupleValueRow'

typealias TupleValueRowArray = Array with TupleValueRow

// One function's analysis: the census rows, and the dense value→row column that finds them.
type FunctionTupleFlow
	export var rows as TupleValueRowArray
	// Dense over the function's value space. `hasRow` is the membership half; `rowAt` is read only where it
	// is true, so no absent-marker value is needed.
	export var hasRow as BoolArray
	export var rowAt as UseTallyArray

	export static function create(valueBound ValueId) returns FunctionTupleFlow
		var hasRow = BoolArray.create()
		hasRow.growFilled(valueBound as ElementIndex, value: false)
		var rowAt = UseTallyArray.create()
		rowAt.growFilled(valueBound as ElementIndex, value: 0)
		return Self{rows: TupleValueRowArray.create(), hasRow: hasRow, rowAt: rowAt}
	end 'create'

	export function tracks(value ValueId) returns bool
		return columnMarks(self.hasRow, value: value)
	end 'tracks'

	export function rowOf(value ValueId) returns TupleValueRow
		let rowIndex = try self.rowAt.get(value) otherwise panic("FunctionTupleFlow.rowOf: rowAt.get OOB at {value} — asked of a value `tracks` answered true for")
		return try self.rows.get(rowIndex) otherwise panic("FunctionTupleFlow.rowOf: rows.get OOB at {rowIndex} — the row-index column is written only by `track`, which pushes the row it indexes")
	end 'rowOf'

	// Begin tracking `value`, or leave the row it already has alone.
	export function track(value ValueId)
		if self.tracks(value) 'alreadyTracked'
			return
		end 'alreadyTracked'

		try self.rowAt.set(value, value: self.rows.count()) otherwise panic("FunctionTupleFlow.track: rowAt.set OOB at {value} against a column of {self.rowAt.count()}")
		try self.hasRow.set(value, value: true) otherwise panic("FunctionTupleFlow.track: hasRow.set OOB at {value} against a column of {self.hasRow.count()}")
		self.rows.push(TupleValueRow.create(value))
	end 'track'
end 'FunctionTupleFlow'

// A function's analysis, or the absence of one: only functions that RETURN a candidate tuple or CALL a
// candidate are analysed, and in most programs that is none of them.
union FunctionTupleFlowSlot
	absent
	present(flow FunctionTupleFlow)
end 'FunctionTupleFlowSlot'

typealias FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray = Array with FunctionTupleFlowSlot

// The absence of an analysis, as a throw rather than a marker value: a function nothing could turn on was
// never walked, and its readers say so at the `try`.
enum NoFunctionFlow
	notAnalysed
end 'NoFunctionFlow'

// What one `ret` of a candidate can do — the callee half of the gate.
union ValueTupleRetKind
	// A fresh box written at both halves and returned: elidable outright.
	freshPair
	// The result of a direct call to a candidate, used by nothing but this return.
	forwarded(calleeIndex FuncIndex)
	// The unreachable zero a range-check panic block returns: both registers get it.
	unreachableZero(zero ValueId)
	// Anything else. The function cannot take the convention.
	unusable
end 'ValueTupleRetKind'

// One recorded `ret` of one candidate.
type ValueTupleRetVerdict
	export let funcIndex as FuncIndex
	export let kind as ValueTupleRetKind

	export static function create(funcIndex FuncIndex, kind ValueTupleRetKind) returns ValueTupleRetVerdict
		return Self{funcIndex: funcIndex, kind: kind}
	end 'create'
end 'ValueTupleRetVerdict'

typealias ValueTupleRetVerdictArray = Array with ValueTupleRetVerdict

// What one CALL SITE of a candidate can do — the caller half of the gate.
union ValueTupleCallShape
	// The halves are read out and the box trivially dropped: no record is needed at this site.
	readThenDropped
	// The pair flows straight into the caller's own return, so the caller must be on the convention too.
	forwardedByCaller
	// Anything else. This site alone takes the callee off the convention.
	unusable
end 'ValueTupleCallShape'

// One recorded call site: which function holds it, which candidate it calls, and what it can do.
type ValueTupleCallSite
	export let callerIndex as FuncIndex
	export let calleeIndex as FuncIndex
	export let shape as ValueTupleCallShape

	export static function create(callerIndex FuncIndex, calleeIndex FuncIndex, shape ValueTupleCallShape) returns ValueTupleCallSite
		return Self{callerIndex: callerIndex, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, shape: shape}
	end 'create'
end 'ValueTupleCallSite'

typealias ValueTupleCallSiteArray = Array with ValueTupleCallSite

// ⭐ **THE PASS.** Whole-module: a callee's convention and its call sites' shapes are one decision, and
// neither end may be judged without the other.
export function applyValueTupleReturn(project Project, module StdModule)
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "applyValueTupleReturn: {module.funcCount()} function(s)")

	// ⚠ **A DUPLICATE FUNCTION NAME DECLINES THE WHOLE PASS**, for `promoteStackRecords`' stated reason:
	// every judgement here resolves a callee by NAME, so two bodies under one name would let a call site be
	// judged against the wrong one. Raising E4015 belongs to `DeadFunctionElimination`, which runs later and
	// sees strictly more collisions.
	match FunctionNameIndex.build(module.functions) 'names'
		duplicateAt then return
		built(byName) then convertWithNameIndex(project, module: module, byName: byName)
	end 'names'
end 'applyValueTupleReturn'

function convertWithNameIndex(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex)
	// PHASE 1 — the structural candidates. Most programs have none, and then nothing below runs.
	var onTheConvention = collectCandidates(project, module: module)
	if not anyCandidate(onTheConvention) 'noTupleReturningFunction'
		return
	end 'noTupleReturningFunction'

	// PHASE 2 — one analysis per function a verdict can turn on, and the two verdict lists read off it.
	let flows = analyseAffectedFunctions(module, byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
	let retVerdicts = collectRetVerdicts(module, flows: flows, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
	let callSites = collectCallSites(module, byName: byName, flows: flows, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)

	// PHASE 3 — remove every candidate one of those lists refuses, until nothing changes.
	settleTheConvention(onTheConvention, retVerdicts: retVerdicts, callSites: callSites)
	if not anyCandidate(onTheConvention) 'everyCandidateWasRefused'
		return
	end 'everyCandidateWasRefused'

	// PHASE 4 — stamp the bit the ABI readers ask, then rewrite both ends against it.
	logTheSettledConvention(module, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
	stampTheConvention(module, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let flow = try flowOf(flows, funcIndex: funcIndex) otherwise continue
		rewriteOneFunction(module, funcIndex: funcIndex, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'convertWithNameIndex'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Phase 1 — the structural candidates
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Which functions came out of the fixpoint on the convention — the one reading that says whether a
// program the pass was expected to change actually changed, without disassembling it.
function logTheSettledConvention(module StdModule, onTheConvention BoolArray)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'notOnIt'
			continue
		end 'notOnIt'

		let name = module.getFunc(funcIndex).name
		logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "applyValueTupleReturn: `{String.from(name)}` returns its pair in two registers")
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'logTheSettledConvention'

function anyCandidate(onTheConvention BoolArray) returns bool
	for on in onTheConvention 'eachFunc'
		if on 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return false
end 'anyCandidate'

// Which functions could take the convention on their DECLARATION alone: a tuple return, no `throws` (the
// secondary slot is the error flag there, and both want the one register), and an address nothing takes.
// ⭐ **THE DECLARATION TEST COMES FIRST AND IS THE PASS'S WHOLE-PROGRAM GATE.** It reads two fields per
// function and walks nothing; the address scan below it is a walk of every op in the module, and a program
// that declares no tuple-returning function must not pay for one. Most programs are that program.
function collectCandidates(project Project, module StdModule) returns BoolArray
	var candidates = BoolArray.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		candidates.push(returnsATupleRecord(project, func: func) and not throwsClauseIsThrowing(func.throwsTypeName))
	end 'eachFunc'

	if not anyCandidate(candidates) 'noTupleReturningFunction'
		return candidates
	end 'noTupleReturningFunction'

	refuseAddressTakenCandidates(project, module: module, candidates: candidates)
	return candidates
end 'collectCandidates'

// ⚠ The two address spellings are asked per CANDIDATE, not per function, because `fnRefThunkName` builds a
// name: asking it of every function would allocate one per function in the program to answer a question
// only a tuple-returning one can be affected by.
function refuseAddressTakenCandidates(project Project, module StdModule, candidates BoolArray)
	let addressTaken = collectAddressTakenFunctions(project, module: module)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto candidates.count() 'eachFunc'
		if not isOnTheConvention(candidates, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'notACandidate'
			continue
		end 'notACandidate'

		let name = module.getFunc(funcIndex).name
		if not addressTaken.contains(name) and not addressTaken.contains(fnRefThunkName(name)) 'addressNeverTaken'
			continue
		end 'addressNeverTaken'

		try candidates.set(funcIndex, value: false) otherwise panic("refuseAddressTakenCandidates: candidates.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'refuseAddressTakenCandidates'

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY FUNCTION WHOSE ADDRESS THIS PROGRAM MATERIALIZES, from the two places an address can come
// from.** A function reached through a POINTER is called by its TYPE, and a function type cannot say which
// return convention its target uses — `StdOp.callIndirect` captures exactly one result, and so does every
// witness dispatch. So an address-taken function keeps the pointer convention.
//
//   • `StdOp.funcAddr` — the op that materializes one. A plain function used as a VALUE names its
//     `__fnref_<name>` env thunk rather than itself, and that thunk does not exist yet at this point in the
//     pipeline, so a candidate asks about BOTH spellings of its own name. An `async` spawn names its target
//     directly and is caught by the same walk without a second rule.
//   • **`.rdata` FUNCTION SLOTS** (`GlobalDataTable.pendingRdataRelocs`) — a witness table's method slots,
//     a layout descriptor's `destroyFunc`/`copyFunc`/`retainFunc`. Those are addresses no op holds, which
//     is exactly why an op walk alone would miss them; `DeadFunctionElimination` roots the same list for
//     the same reason.
function collectAddressTakenFunctions(project Project, module StdModule) returns FnRefThunkSet
	var taken = FnRefThunkSet.create()
	// ⚠ HOISTED, because this walk is WHOLE-MODULE: `liveOpIndices` mints a fresh column per call, which
	// over every function of the program is one allocation per function for an answer discarded at the end
	// of the iteration. `collectLiveOpIndices` refills this one, keeping its capacity.
	var liveOps = OpIndexArray.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		module.collectLiveOpIndices(module.getFunc(funcIndex), into: liveOps)
		for opIdx in liveOps 'eachOp'
			noteAddressTakenByOp(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectAddressTakenFunctions: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), taken: taken)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'

	for reloc in project.globalData.pendingRdataRelocs 'eachRdataSlot'
		taken.insert(reloc.funcName)
	end 'eachRdataSlot'
	return taken
end 'collectAddressTakenFunctions'

function noteAddressTakenByOp(op StdOp, taken FnRefThunkSet)
	match op 'op'
		const to osExit then break 'op'
		funcAddr(_, funcName) then taken.insert(funcName)
		callIndirect to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteAddressTakenByOp'

// Is this function's declared return a TUPLE record? A tuple is a synthesized struct, so it arrives as a
// `structRef` at a `__Tuple…` name (`ProgramSignatures.internTupleType`). A name the project's interner
// cannot resolve is not one this pass may reason about.
function returnsATupleRecord(project Project, func IrFunction) returns bool
	return match func.maxonReturnType 'declared'
		void gives false
		value(inner) gives typeIsATupleRecord(project, t: inner)
	end 'declared'
end 'returnsATupleRecord'

function typeIsATupleRecord(project Project, t MaxonType) returns bool
	if maxonTypeTag(t) != ValueTypeTag.structRef 'notARecordName'
		return false
	end 'notARecordName'

	let name = try project.typeNames.nameOf(typeNameIdOf(t)) otherwise return false
	return isTupleTypeName(name)
end 'typeIsATupleRecord'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Phase 2 — the per-function census
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Analyse only the functions a verdict can turn on: a candidate (whose own returns are judged) and any
// function holding a direct call to one (whose call sites are). Everything else stays `absent`, which in
// most programs is every function there is.
function analyseAffectedFunctions(module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray
	var flows = FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray.create()
	// Hoisted for `collectAddressTakenFunctions`' reason: the screen below is whole-module.
	var liveOps = OpIndexArray.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		module.collectLiveOpIndices(func, into: liveOps)
		if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex) and not callsACandidate(module, liveOps: liveOps, byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention) 'nothingToJudgeHere'
			flows.push(FunctionTupleFlowSlot.absent)
			continue
		end 'nothingToJudgeHere'

		flows.push(FunctionTupleFlowSlot.present(analyseOneFunction(module, func: func, liveOps: liveOps, byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)))
	end 'eachFunc'
	return flows
end 'analyseAffectedFunctions'

function isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
	return try onTheConvention.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("isOnTheConvention: onTheConvention.get OOB at {funcIndex} — the column has one entry per module function")
end 'isOnTheConvention'

function flowOf(flows FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns FunctionTupleFlow throws NoFunctionFlow
	match try flows.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("flowOf: flows.get OOB at {funcIndex} — the column has one entry per module function") 'slot'
		absent then throw NoFunctionFlow.notAnalysed
		present(flow) then return flow
	end 'slot'
end 'flowOf'

function callsACandidate(module StdModule, liveOps OpIndexArray, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns bool
	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachOp'
		if opCallsACandidate(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("callsACandidate: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention) 'found'
			return true
		end 'found'
	end 'eachOp'
	return false
end 'callsACandidate'

function opCallsACandidate(op StdOp, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns bool
	return match op 'op'
		const to param gives false
		call(_, callee, _) gives isCandidateCallee(byName, callee: callee, onTheConvention: onTheConvention)
		retVoid to osUnpublishNamedSegment gives false
	end 'op'
end 'opCallsACandidate'

function isCandidateCallee(byName FunctionNameIndex, callee ByteArray, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns bool
	let funcIndex = try byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise return false
	return isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex)
end 'isCandidateCallee'

// ⭐ **ONE PASS TO TRACK, ONE TO CLASSIFY, AND THE CLASSIFIER FAILS CLOSED.** Every value this pass could
// rewrite gets a row first — the returned values and the results of `__mm_alloc` and of calls to
// candidates — so no later reader can meet a value whose row does not exist yet. Then every op and every
// BRANCH EDGE is read once. A read the classifier has no arm for lands in `other`, which refuses the
// value, so an op variant appended tomorrow costs a missed rewrite and never a wrong one.
function analyseOneFunction(module StdModule, func IrFunction, liveOps OpIndexArray, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns FunctionTupleFlow
	var flow = FunctionTupleFlow.create(nextFreeValueId(module, func: func))

	// ⚠ THE RETURNED VALUES FIRST, AND SEPARATELY. A `const` gets a row only where a `ret` already named it
	// (every function holds constants, and a row per constant would make the census as large as the code) —
	// so the walk that stamps origins has to run against a table the returns have already seeded.
	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachReturn'
		trackReturnedValue(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyseOneFunction: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), flow: flow)
	end 'eachReturn'

	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachDef'
		trackValueOrigin(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyseOneFunction: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, flow: flow)
	end 'eachDef'

	var readByOneOp = StdValueUseSet.createRecordingIds()
	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachUse'
		classifyOneOp(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("analyseOneFunction: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), flow: flow, readByOneOp: readByOneOp)
	end 'eachUse'

	// ⚠ BRANCH-EDGE ARGS LIVE ON THE BLOCK, NOT ON AN OP, so `collectStdOpUses` structurally cannot see them
	// and says so. A pair reaching a merge is refused: the merge would need a record to name.
	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		let block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("analyseOneFunction: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
				noteOtherUse(flow, value: argId)
			end 'eachArg'
		end 'eachEdge'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return flow
end 'analyseOneFunction'

// A `ret`'s operand is tracked wherever it comes from, so a returned parameter gets a row whose origin
// stays `elsewhere` and is refused BY NAME at `retKindOf` rather than by there being no row.
function trackReturnedValue(op StdOp, flow FunctionTupleFlow)
	match op 'op'
		const to branch then break 'op'
		ret(retValId) then flow.track(retValId)
		param to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'trackReturnedValue'

// Stamp where a tracked value came from, and give a row to the call results a rewrite could name.
function trackValueOrigin(op StdOp, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray, flow FunctionTupleFlow)
	match op 'op'
		const(resultId, _, _) then trackConstant(resultId, flow: flow)
		binOp to branch then break 'op'
		ret then break 'op'
		param then break 'op'
		call(resultId, callee, args) then trackCallResult(resultId, callee: callee, args: args, byName: byName, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, flow: flow)
		retVoid to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'trackValueOrigin'

function trackConstant(resultId ValueId, flow FunctionTupleFlow)
	if not flow.tracks(resultId) 'noReturnNamesIt'
		return
	end 'noReturnNamesIt'
	var row = flow.rowOf(resultId)
	row.origin = TupleValueOrigin.constant
end 'trackConstant'

function trackCallResult(resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, byName FunctionNameIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray, flow FunctionTupleFlow)
	// ⚠ Like a constant, a BOX gets a row only where a `ret` already named it — one that is not returned can
	// never be a fresh-pair return, and giving every allocation a row would make the classifier's per-op
	// scan of the rows grow with the function's allocation count.
	if callee.equals(MmAllocName) and args.count() >= MmAllocMinArgCount 'aHeapBox'
		if not flow.tracks(resultId) 'noReturnNamesIt'
			return
		end 'noReturnNamesIt'

		var box = flow.rowOf(resultId)
		box.origin = TupleValueOrigin.freshBox
		return
	end 'aHeapBox'

	let calleeIndex = try byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise return
	if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: calleeIndex) 'notACandidate'
		return
	end 'notACandidate'

	flow.track(resultId)
	var pair = flow.rowOf(resultId)
	pair.origin = TupleValueOrigin.directCall(calleeIndex)
end 'trackCallResult'

// Read one op's uses into the census: the four rewritable shapes are tallied by name, and every other read
// the op makes is refused through `collectStdOpUses` — the dialect's one "what does this op read?" oracle.
function classifyOneOp(op StdOp, flow FunctionTupleFlow, readByOneOp StdValueUseSet)
	match op 'op'
		const to branch then break 'op'
		ret(retValId) then noteReturnedValue(flow, retValId: retValId)
		param then break 'op'
		call(_, callee, args) then noteTrivialDrop(flow, callee: callee, args: args)
		retVoid or globalAddr then break 'op'
		loadIndirect(_, addrId, offset, valueType) then noteHalfLoad(flow, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) then noteHalfStore(flow, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, valueId: valueId, valueType: valueType)
		osAllocPages to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'

	// ⚠ **OVER THE OP'S READS, NEVER OVER THE CENSUS ROWS.** The two enumerate the same intersection, but
	// a row scan per op is O(ops x rows) and both grow with the function — a body that returns and calls a
	// tuple-returning function many times is then quadratic in its own size. `createRecordingIds` is what
	// makes the op's own reads enumerable, and its sparse `clear` is what makes the per-op refill cheap.
	readByOneOp.clear()
	collectStdOpUses(op, referenced: readByOneOp)
	for readIndex in 0 upto readByOneOp.insertedIdCount() 'eachRead'
		let value = readByOneOp.insertedIdAt(readIndex)
		if opAccountsForTheValue(op, value: value) 'alreadyTallied'
			continue
		end 'alreadyTallied'

		noteOtherUse(flow, value: value)
	end 'eachRead'
end 'classifyOneOp'

// Whether the arm above already tallied this op's read of `value` — the exact complement of the catch-all,
// written once so the two cannot come to disagree about which reads are accounted for.
function opAccountsForTheValue(op StdOp, value ValueId) returns bool
	return match op 'op'
		const to branch gives false
		ret(retValId) gives retValId == value
		param gives false
		call(_, callee, args) gives callIsTheTrivialDropOf(callee, args: args, value: value)
		retVoid or globalAddr gives false
		loadIndirect(_, addrId, offset, valueType) gives addrId == value and isAWholeHalf(offset, valueType: valueType)
		storeIndirect(addrId, offset, valueId, valueType) gives addrId == value and valueId != value and isAWholeHalf(offset, valueType: valueType)
		osAllocPages to osUnpublishNamedSegment gives false
	end 'op'
end 'opAccountsForTheValue'

// An access at offset 0 or 8, one whole machine word wide. Anything narrower, wider or misaligned is not a
// half of the pair — and a FLOAT half is refused here too, because the secondary return slot is a
// general-purpose register and an `f64` virtual constrained to it would cross register files.
function isAWholeHalf(offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType) returns bool
	if valueType != StdType.i64 'notAWholeIntegralWord'
		return false
	end 'notAWholeIntegralWord'
	return offset == LowHalfOffset or offset == HighHalfOffset
end 'isAWholeHalf'

function callIsTheTrivialDropOf(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, value ValueId) returns bool
	let dropped = try trivialDropOf(callee, args: args) otherwise return false
	return dropped == value
end 'callIsTheTrivialDropOf'

function noteOtherUse(flow FunctionTupleFlow, value ValueId)
	if not flow.tracks(value) 'notOurs'
		return
	end 'notOurs'
	var row = flow.rowOf(value)
	row.other = row.other + 1
end 'noteOtherUse'

function noteReturnedValue(flow FunctionTupleFlow, retValId ValueId)
	var row = flow.rowOf(retValId)
	row.returnUses = row.returnUses + 1
end 'noteReturnedValue'

function noteTrivialDrop(flow FunctionTupleFlow, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray)
	let dropped = try trivialDropOf(callee, args: args) otherwise return
	if not flow.tracks(dropped) 'notOurs'
		return
	end 'notOurs'
	var row = flow.rowOf(dropped)
	row.trivialDrops = row.trivialDrops + 1
end 'noteTrivialDrop'

function noteHalfLoad(flow FunctionTupleFlow, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueType StdType)
	if not flow.tracks(addrId) or not isAWholeHalf(offset, valueType: valueType) 'notAHalfOfATrackedPair'
		return
	end 'notAHalfOfATrackedPair'

	var row = flow.rowOf(addrId)
	if offset == LowHalfOffset 'low'
		row.loadLow = row.loadLow + 1
		return
	end 'low'
	row.loadHigh = row.loadHigh + 1
end 'noteHalfLoad'

function noteHalfStore(flow FunctionTupleFlow, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, valueType StdType)
	if not flow.tracks(addrId) or valueId == addrId or not isAWholeHalf(offset, valueType: valueType) 'notAHalfOfATrackedPair'
		return
	end 'notAHalfOfATrackedPair'

	var row = flow.rowOf(addrId)
	if offset == LowHalfOffset 'low'
		row.storeLow = row.storeLow + 1
		row.storedLow = valueId
		return
	end 'low'
	row.storeHigh = row.storeHigh + 1
	row.storedHigh = valueId
end 'noteHalfStore'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Phase 2b — the two verdict lists
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

function collectRetVerdicts(module StdModule, flows FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns ValueTupleRetVerdictArray
	var verdicts = ValueTupleRetVerdictArray.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'notACandidate'
			continue
		end 'notACandidate'

		let flow = try flowOf(flows, funcIndex: funcIndex) otherwise panic("collectRetVerdicts: function {funcIndex} is a candidate and so was analysed — `analyseAffectedFunctions` analyses every candidate")
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
			noteRetVerdict(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectRetVerdicts: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), funcIndex: funcIndex, flow: flow, verdicts: verdicts)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return verdicts
end 'collectRetVerdicts'

function noteRetVerdict(op StdOp, funcIndex FuncIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, verdicts ValueTupleRetVerdictArray)
	match op 'op'
		const to branch then break 'op'
		ret(retValId) then verdicts.push(ValueTupleRetVerdict.create(funcIndex, kind: retKindOf(flow, retValId: retValId)))
		param to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteRetVerdict'

// ⭐ **THE CALLEE HALF OF THE GATE**, and the admitted shapes are exactly this file's header's two plus the
// unreachable panic return. Everything else — a returned parameter, a returned record something else also
// reads — is `unusable`, which is what keeps a value-convention body free of any record whose ownership
// would have to move word by word.
function retKindOf(flow FunctionTupleFlow, retValId ValueId) returns ValueTupleRetKind
	if not flow.tracks(retValId) 'noRowAtAll'
		return ValueTupleRetKind.unusable
	end 'noRowAtAll'

	let row = flow.rowOf(retValId)
	return match row.origin 'origin'
		freshBox gives freshPairKindOf(row)
		directCall(calleeIndex) gives forwardedKindOf(row, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
		constant gives unreachableZeroKindOf(row, retValId: retValId)
		elsewhere gives ValueTupleRetKind.unusable
	end 'origin'
end 'retKindOf'

function freshPairKindOf(row TupleValueRow) returns ValueTupleRetKind
	if not row.isAnUnsharedFreshPair() 'somethingElseSeesTheBox'
		return ValueTupleRetKind.unusable
	end 'somethingElseSeesTheBox'
	return ValueTupleRetKind.freshPair
end 'freshPairKindOf'

function unreachableZeroKindOf(row TupleValueRow, retValId ValueId) returns ValueTupleRetKind
	if not row.isAnUnreachableZero() 'theConstantIsAlsoReadElsewhere'
		return ValueTupleRetKind.unusable
	end 'theConstantIsAlsoReadElsewhere'
	return ValueTupleRetKind.unreachableZero(retValId)
end 'unreachableZeroKindOf'

function forwardedKindOf(row TupleValueRow, calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueTupleRetKind
	if not row.isForwardedToTheReturn() 'thePairIsAlsoReadHere'
		return ValueTupleRetKind.unusable
	end 'thePairIsAlsoReadHere'
	return ValueTupleRetKind.forwarded(calleeIndex)
end 'forwardedKindOf'

// Every direct call to a candidate, wherever it is, with what that site can do.
function collectCallSites(module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex, flows FunctionTupleFlowSlotArray, onTheConvention BoolArray) returns ValueTupleCallSiteArray
	var sites = ValueTupleCallSiteArray.create()
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let flow = try flowOf(flows, funcIndex: funcIndex) otherwise continue
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
			noteCallSite(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("collectCallSites: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), funcIndex: funcIndex, byName: byName, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, sites: sites)
		end 'eachOp'
	end 'eachFunc'
	return sites
end 'collectCallSites'

function noteCallSite(op StdOp, funcIndex FuncIndex, byName FunctionNameIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray, sites ValueTupleCallSiteArray)
	match op 'op'
		const to param then break 'op'
		call(resultId, callee, _) then noteCandidateCallSite(resultId, callee: callee, funcIndex: funcIndex, byName: byName, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, sites: sites)
		retVoid to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteCallSite'

function noteCandidateCallSite(resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, funcIndex FuncIndex, byName FunctionNameIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray, sites ValueTupleCallSiteArray)
	let calleeIndex = try byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise return
	if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: calleeIndex) 'notACandidate'
		return
	end 'notACandidate'

	sites.push(ValueTupleCallSite.create(funcIndex, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, shape: callShapeOf(flow, pair: resultId, callerIsACandidate: isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex))))
end 'noteCandidateCallSite'

// ⭐ **THE CALLER HALF OF THE GATE.** A pair that is read out and trivially dropped needs no record; one
// that flows into the caller's own return needs none either, PROVIDED the caller is on the convention.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A CALLER THAT IS NOT A CANDIDATE FORWARDS NOTHING, AND THE REFUSAL HAS TO BE STATED HERE.** The
// fixpoint carries "the caller fell, so the callee falls" along an edge between two CANDIDATES; a caller
// the declaration test already excluded — one whose address is taken, one that throws — is never a node
// that edge can be seeded at, so admitting its site would leave the callee on the convention with a
// forwarding `ret` in a function whose ABI hands back one register. Then `rewriteOneReturn` writes the
// low half where the caller's type says a record pointer, and the indirect call reading it faults.
function callShapeOf(flow FunctionTupleFlow, pair ValueId, callerIsACandidate bool) returns ValueTupleCallShape
	if not flow.tracks(pair) 'noRowAtAll'
		return ValueTupleCallShape.unusable
	end 'noRowAtAll'

	let row = flow.rowOf(pair)
	if row.isReadThenDropped() 'noRecordNeeded'
		return ValueTupleCallShape.readThenDropped
	end 'noRecordNeeded'
	if row.isForwardedToTheReturn() and callerIsACandidate 'straightBackOut'
		return ValueTupleCallShape.forwardedByCaller
	end 'straightBackOut'
	return ValueTupleCallShape.unusable
end 'callShapeOf'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Phase 3 — the fixpoint
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ⭐ **WHICH CANDIDATES FALL WHEN ONE FALLS.** Both verdict lists say the same kind of thing — *this
// candidate is refused if THAT function is*: a `ret` that forwards a pair depends on the callee it forwards
// from, and a call site that forwards into its caller's own return depends on that caller. So the removal is
// a transitive closure over ONE relation, seeded by the refusals that depend on nothing — the shape
// `reverseReachabilityMark` answers in O(candidates + edges) and a re-scan until nothing changes answers at
// one pass per LINK of the longest refusal chain, which is quadratic in a program of forwarding wrappers.
function settleTheConvention(onTheConvention BoolArray, retVerdicts ValueTupleRetVerdictArray, callSites ValueTupleCallSiteArray)
	var dependedOn = CsrColumn.create()
	var dependent = CsrColumn.create()
	collectDependencyEdges(dependedOn, dependent: dependent, retVerdicts: retVerdicts, callSites: callSites)

	var seeds = BoolArray.create()
	seeds.growFilled(onTheConvention.count(), value: false)
	seedRefusals(seeds, retVerdicts: retVerdicts, callSites: callSites)

	let refused = reverseReachabilityMark(buildCsr(dependedOn, to: dependent, nodeCount: onTheConvention.count() as CsrIndex), seeds: seeds)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto onTheConvention.count() 'eachFunc'
		if not (try refused.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("settleTheConvention: refused.get OOB at {funcIndex} — the mark spans the same function count the seeds did")) 'kept'
			continue
		end 'kept'

		try onTheConvention.set(funcIndex, value: false) otherwise panic("settleTheConvention: onTheConvention.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'settleTheConvention'

// One edge per dependency, as the parallel columns `buildCsr` takes: a `ret` that forwards depends on the
// callee it forwards from, and a call site that forwards into its caller's return depends on that caller.
function collectDependencyEdges(dependedOn CsrColumn, dependent CsrColumn, retVerdicts ValueTupleRetVerdictArray, callSites ValueTupleCallSiteArray)
	for verdict in retVerdicts 'eachReturn'
		match verdict.kind 'kind'
			freshPair then break 'kind'
			forwarded(calleeIndex) then noteDependency(dependedOn, dependent: dependent, dependedOnIndex: calleeIndex, dependentIndex: verdict.funcIndex)
			unreachableZero then break 'kind'
			unusable then break 'kind'
		end 'kind'
	end 'eachReturn'

	for site in callSites 'eachSite'
		match site.shape 'shape'
			readThenDropped then break 'shape'
			forwardedByCaller then noteDependency(dependedOn, dependent: dependent, dependedOnIndex: site.callerIndex, dependentIndex: site.calleeIndex)
			unusable then break 'shape'
		end 'shape'
	end 'eachSite'
end 'collectDependencyEdges'

function noteDependency(dependedOn CsrColumn, dependent CsrColumn, dependedOnIndex FuncIndex, dependentIndex FuncIndex)
	dependedOn.push(dependedOnIndex as CsrIndex)
	dependent.push(dependentIndex as CsrIndex)
end 'noteDependency'

// The refusals that depend on nothing: a `ret` no rewrite fits, and a call site no rewrite fits.
function seedRefusals(seeds BoolArray, retVerdicts ValueTupleRetVerdictArray, callSites ValueTupleCallSiteArray)
	for verdict in retVerdicts 'eachReturn'
		match verdict.kind 'kind'
			freshPair then break 'kind'
			forwarded then break 'kind'
			unreachableZero then break 'kind'
			unusable then markSeed(seeds, funcIndex: verdict.funcIndex)
		end 'kind'
	end 'eachReturn'

	for site in callSites 'eachSite'
		match site.shape 'shape'
			readThenDropped then break 'shape'
			forwardedByCaller then break 'shape'
			unusable then markSeed(seeds, funcIndex: site.calleeIndex)
		end 'shape'
	end 'eachSite'
end 'seedRefusals'

function markSeed(seeds BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex)
	try seeds.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("markSeed: seeds.set OOB at {funcIndex} against a column of {seeds.count()}")
end 'markSeed'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Phase 4 — the rewrite
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ⭐ **THE BIT EVERY ABI READER ASKS.** The two return emitters in `LowerMaxonToStd` have already run, so
// this pass writes the `errorReturn` itself; what the bit is for is the readers BELOW it — wasm's
// `funcSignatureResultValTypes`, whose functype must declare the second result the body now writes, or the
// module does not validate.
function stampTheConvention(module StdModule, onTheConvention BoolArray)
	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'notOnIt'
			continue
		end 'notOnIt'

		var func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
		func.usesValueTupleReturn = true
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'stampTheConvention'

// The rewrite plan for one function: which values leave through the two return registers, which boxes stop
// being built, which call results now arrive as a register pair, and the second value id each pair is
// captured into.
//
// The three marks are not alternatives — a fresh pair is BOTH elided and returned as a pair, and a
// captured pair whose one use is the return is BOTH a register pair and returned as one — so each op's
// rewrite asks the mark that governs IT rather than a single classification.
type ValueTuplePlan
	// `ret v` becomes `errorReturn(lowOf[v], highOf[v])`.
	export var returnsAsAPair as BoolArray
	// The `__mm_alloc` defining this value, and every store into it, disappear.
	export var elidedBox as BoolArray
	// The `call` defining this value becomes a `tryCall` capturing both registers; its half loads become
	// reads of those two values and its trivial drop disappears.
	export var registerPair as BoolArray
	export var lowOf as ValueIdArray
	export var highOf as ValueIdArray
	export var anyRewrite as bool
	// Whether the fixpoint left THIS function on the convention. Only such a function may hand back two
	// registers, and `markRegisterPair` marks a captured pair as returnable without knowing which function
	// holds it — so the ABI rewrite asks here before it writes.
	export let onTheConvention as bool

	export static function create(valueBound ValueId, onTheConvention bool) returns ValueTuplePlan
		return Self{returnsAsAPair: markColumn(valueBound), elidedBox: markColumn(valueBound), registerPair: markColumn(valueBound), lowOf: halfColumn(valueBound), highOf: halfColumn(valueBound), anyRewrite: false, onTheConvention: onTheConvention}
	end 'create'

	export function returnsAPair(value ValueId) returns bool
		return columnMarks(self.returnsAsAPair, value: value)
	end 'returnsAPair'

	export function isAnElidedBox(value ValueId) returns bool
		return columnMarks(self.elidedBox, value: value)
	end 'isAnElidedBox'

	export function isARegisterPair(value ValueId) returns bool
		return columnMarks(self.registerPair, value: value)
	end 'isARegisterPair'

	export function lowHalfOf(value ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.lowOf.get(value) otherwise panic("ValueTuplePlan.lowHalfOf: lowOf.get OOB at {value} — asked of a value a mark column answered true for")
	end 'lowHalfOf'

	export function highHalfOf(value ValueId) returns ValueId
		return try self.highOf.get(value) otherwise panic("ValueTuplePlan.highHalfOf: highOf.get OOB at {value} — asked of a value a mark column answered true for")
	end 'highHalfOf'

	// The FRESH-LITERAL return: the box stops being built and its two stored words leave in the registers.
	export function markFreshPair(box ValueId, low ValueId, high ValueId)
		self.mark(self.elidedBox, value: box)
		self.markPairHalves(box, low: low, high: high)
	end 'markFreshPair'

	// The unreachable panic return: both registers take the same zero. Nothing reads either, and minting a
	// second constant for a block that never runs would be one more op to keep in step with the first.
	export function markUnreachableZero(zero ValueId)
		self.markPairHalves(zero, low: zero, high: zero)
	end 'markUnreachableZero'

	// A captured pair's LOW half is the call's own result id, so only the high half is minted — which is
	// what keeps every existing reader of the result naming the value it already named.
	export function markRegisterPair(pair ValueId, high ValueId)
		self.mark(self.registerPair, value: pair)
		self.markPairHalves(pair, low: pair, high: high)
	end 'markRegisterPair'

	function markPairHalves(value ValueId, low ValueId, high ValueId)
		self.mark(self.returnsAsAPair, value: value)
		try self.lowOf.set(value, value: low) otherwise panic("ValueTuplePlan.markPairHalves: lowOf.set OOB at {value}")
		try self.highOf.set(value, value: high) otherwise panic("ValueTuplePlan.markPairHalves: highOf.set OOB at {value}")
	end 'markPairHalves'

	function mark(column BoolArray, value ValueId)
		try column.set(value, value: true) otherwise panic("ValueTuplePlan.mark: column.set OOB at {value} against a column of {column.count()}")
		self.anyRewrite = true
	end 'mark'
end 'ValueTuplePlan'

function markColumn(valueBound ValueId) returns BoolArray
	var column = BoolArray.create()
	column.growFilled(valueBound as ElementIndex, value: false)
	return column
end 'markColumn'

function halfColumn(valueBound ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
	var column = ValueIdArray.create()
	column.growFilled(valueBound as ElementIndex, value: 0)
	return column
end 'halfColumn'

function rewriteOneFunction(module StdModule, funcIndex FuncIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray)
	let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)
	let liveOps = module.liveOpIndices(func)
	// The value space, read ONCE: it is a walk of the function, and the minter and the plan's columns are
	// two readings of the same number.
	let valueBound = nextFreeValueId(module, func: func)
	var mint = ValueMinter.create(valueBound as ParamCount)
	var plan = ValueTuplePlan.create(valueBound, onTheConvention: isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex))

	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachOp'
		planOneOp(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteOneFunction: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), funcIndex: funcIndex, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, plan: plan, mint: mint)
	end 'eachOp'

	if not plan.anyRewrite 'nothingToDoHere'
		return
	end 'nothingToDoHere'

	var subst = ValueIdArray.create()
	var doomed = DoomedOpSet.create()
	for opIdx in liveOps 'eachOp'
		rewriteOneOp(module, opIdx: opIdx, plan: plan, subst: subst, doomed: doomed)
	end 'eachOp'

	// The substitution must land before the removal: it walks the LIVE ops, and the loads it retires are
	// still among them until `removeDoomedOps` takes them out.
	substituteValuesInFunc(module, func: func, subst: subst)
	removeDoomedOps(module, func: func, doomed: doomed)
	removeConstantsNothingReadsAnyMore(module, func: func)
end 'rewriteOneFunction'

// ⭐ **THE SIZE AND THE DESTRUCTOR OF A BOX THAT IS NO LONGER BUILT.** Deleting an `__mm_alloc` orphans the
// two `const`s it read, and this pass is the LAST in the pipeline — no later fold retires them — so they
// would reach the backend as two `mov reg, imm` writing a register nothing reads, in the very functions
// this rung exists to make cheaper.
//
// It runs only in a function this pass actually rewrote, so the constants it sweeps are the ones it
// orphaned plus whatever was already dead in that one function; the removal itself is `removeOpsDefining`,
// the one block rebuild every Std pass that retires a def shares.
function removeConstantsNothingReadsAnyMore(module StdModule, func IrFunction)
	var stillRead = StdValueUseSet.create()
	collectFunctionValueUses(module, func: func, referenced: stillRead)

	var orphaned = StdValueUseSet.create()
	for opIdx in module.liveOpIndices(func) 'eachOp'
		noteOrphanedConstant(try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("removeConstantsNothingReadsAnyMore: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops"), stillRead: stillRead, orphaned: orphaned)
	end 'eachOp'
	removeOpsDefining(module, func: func, doomed: orphaned)
end 'removeConstantsNothingReadsAnyMore'

function noteOrphanedConstant(op StdOp, stillRead StdValueUseSet, orphaned StdValueUseSet)
	match op 'op'
		const(resultId, _, _) then noteUnreadValue(resultId, stillRead: stillRead, orphaned: orphaned)
		binOp to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'noteOrphanedConstant'

function noteUnreadValue(resultId ValueId, stillRead StdValueUseSet, orphaned StdValueUseSet)
	if stillRead.contains(resultId) 'somethingReadsIt'
		return
	end 'somethingReadsIt'
	orphaned.insert(resultId)
end 'noteUnreadValue'

// Decide what happens to one op's value. Both marks come off the SETTLED verdict, so a shape the fixpoint
// refused cannot be reached here — and a `ret` of a candidate that is neither admitted shape is an
// internal disagreement rather than a program this pass declines, which is why it panics.
function planOneOp(op StdOp, funcIndex FuncIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray, plan ValueTuplePlan, mint ValueMinter)
	match op 'op'
		const to branch then break 'op'
		ret(retValId) then planOneReturn(retValId, funcIndex: funcIndex, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, plan: plan)
		param then break 'op'
		call(resultId, _, _) then planOneCallResult(resultId, flow: flow, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, plan: plan, mint: mint)
		retVoid to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'planOneOp'

function planOneReturn(retValId ValueId, funcIndex FuncIndex, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray, plan ValueTuplePlan)
	if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'theHeapConvention'
		return
	end 'theHeapConvention'

	match retKindOf(flow, retValId: retValId) 'kind'
		freshPair then markTheFreshPair(retValId, flow: flow, plan: plan)
		// Both registers take the same zero: nothing reads either, and inventing a second constant for a
		// block that never runs would be a second op to keep in step with the first.
		unreachableZero(zero) then plan.markUnreachableZero(zero)
		// The call site's own plan entry has already captured the pair into two registers; this return
		// merely reads them, and `rewriteOneOp` finds it through the `registerPair` mark.
		forwarded then break 'kind'
		unusable then panic("planOneReturn: function {funcIndex} is on the value-tuple convention but one of its returns is neither a fresh pair nor a forwarded one — `settleTheConvention` removes exactly such a candidate before this runs")
	end 'kind'
end 'planOneReturn'

function markTheFreshPair(retValId ValueId, flow FunctionTupleFlow, plan ValueTuplePlan)
	let row = flow.rowOf(retValId)
	plan.markFreshPair(retValId, low: row.storedLow, high: row.storedHigh)
end 'markTheFreshPair'

function planOneCallResult(resultId ValueId, flow FunctionTupleFlow, onTheConvention BoolArray, plan ValueTuplePlan, mint ValueMinter)
	if not flow.tracks(resultId) 'notOurs'
		return
	end 'notOurs'

	match flow.rowOf(resultId).origin 'origin'
		freshBox then break 'origin'
		directCall(calleeIndex) then markThePairIfTheCalleeKeptTheConvention(resultId, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, onTheConvention: onTheConvention, plan: plan, mint: mint)
		constant then break 'origin'
		elsewhere then break 'origin'
	end 'origin'
end 'planOneCallResult'

function markThePairIfTheCalleeKeptTheConvention(resultId ValueId, calleeIndex FuncIndex, onTheConvention BoolArray, plan ValueTuplePlan, mint ValueMinter)
	if not isOnTheConvention(onTheConvention, funcIndex: calleeIndex) 'refusedByTheFixpoint'
		return
	end 'refusedByTheFixpoint'
	plan.markRegisterPair(resultId, high: mint.mint())
end 'markThePairIfTheCalleeKeptTheConvention'

// Rewrite one op in place, or doom it. A `ret` may be a TERMINATOR or the rare mid-block body op, so both
// are reached through the one live-op walk and rewritten by `ops.set` at the index they already occupy —
// a terminator names its op by index, so nothing else has to move.
function rewriteOneOp(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, plan ValueTuplePlan, subst ValueIdArray, doomed DoomedOpSet)
	match try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("rewriteOneOp: live op index {opIdx} not in module.ops") 'op'
		const to branch then break 'op'
		ret(retValId) then rewriteOneReturn(module, opIdx: opIdx, retValId: retValId, plan: plan)
		param then break 'op'
		call(resultId, callee, args) then rewriteOneCall(module, opIdx: opIdx, resultId: resultId, callee: callee, args: args, plan: plan, doomed: doomed)
		retVoid or globalAddr then break 'op'
		loadIndirect(resultId, addrId, offset, _) then rewriteOneHalfLoad(opIdx, resultId: resultId, addrId: addrId, offset: offset, plan: plan, subst: subst, doomed: doomed)
		storeIndirect(addrId, _, _, _) then doomAStoreIntoAnElidedBox(opIdx, addrId: addrId, plan: plan, doomed: doomed)
		osAllocPages to osUnpublishNamedSegment then break 'op'
	end 'op'
end 'rewriteOneOp'

function rewriteOneReturn(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, retValId ValueId, plan ValueTuplePlan)
	if not plan.returnsAPair(retValId) 'anOrdinaryReturn'
		return
	end 'anOrdinaryReturn'

	if not plan.onTheConvention 'theHeapConvention'
		panic("rewriteOneReturn: value {retValId} is returned as a register pair by a function the fixpoint left on the HEAP convention — its declared one-register return would hand back the low half where a record pointer is expected; `callShapeOf` refuses a forwarding site whose caller is not a candidate so this cannot be reached")
	end 'theHeapConvention'

	try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: StdOp.errorReturn(plan.lowHalfOf(retValId), errorFlagId: plan.highHalfOf(retValId))) otherwise panic("rewriteOneReturn: ops.set({opIdx}) OOB")
end 'rewriteOneReturn'

function rewriteOneCall(module StdModule, opIdx OpIndex, resultId ValueId, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, plan ValueTuplePlan, doomed DoomedOpSet)
	if plan.isARegisterPair(resultId) 'capturesBothRegisters'
		try module.ops.set(opIdx, value: StdOp.tryCall(resultId, errorFlagId: plan.highHalfOf(resultId), callee: callee, args: args)) otherwise panic("rewriteOneCall: ops.set({opIdx}) OOB")
		return
	end 'capturesBothRegisters'

	if callee.equals(MmAllocName) and plan.isAnElidedBox(resultId) 'theBoxThatIsNoLongerBuilt'
		doomed.insert(opIdx)
		return
	end 'theBoxThatIsNoLongerBuilt'

	let dropped = try trivialDropOf(callee, args: args) otherwise return
	if plan.isARegisterPair(dropped) 'theDropOfANonExistentRecord'
		doomed.insert(opIdx)
	end 'theDropOfANonExistentRecord'
end 'rewriteOneCall'

function rewriteOneHalfLoad(opIdx OpIndex, resultId ValueId, addrId ValueId, offset ByteOffset, plan ValueTuplePlan, subst ValueIdArray, doomed DoomedOpSet)
	if not plan.isARegisterPair(addrId) 'notAHalfOfACapturedPair'
		return
	end 'notAHalfOfACapturedPair'

	let half = plan.lowHalfOf(addrId) if offset == LowHalfOffset else plan.highHalfOf(addrId)
	recordValueSubstitution(subst, resultId: resultId, replacement: half)
	doomed.insert(opIdx)
end 'rewriteOneHalfLoad'

function doomAStoreIntoAnElidedBox(opIdx OpIndex, addrId ValueId, plan ValueTuplePlan, doomed DoomedOpSet)
	if not plan.isAnElidedBox(addrId) 'notTheBoxThatIsNoLongerBuilt'
		return
	end 'notTheBoxThatIsNoLongerBuilt'
	doomed.insert(opIdx)
end 'doomAStoreIntoAnElidedBox'

// ⚠ A TERMINATOR IS NEVER DOOMED — a `ret` is REWRITTEN into an `errorReturn` at the same index — so
// rebuilding `opRefs` alone reaches every removal, exactly as `applyPromotions` states for its own.
function removeDoomedOps(module StdModule, func IrFunction, doomed DoomedOpSet)
	if doomed.count() == 0 'nothingDoomed'
		return
	end 'nothingDoomed'

	for blockRef in func.blockRefs 'eachBlock'
		var block = try module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("removeDoomedOps: blockRef from func.blockRefs not in module.blocks")
		var kept = OpIndexArray.create()
		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachRef'
			if doomed.contains(opIdx) 'removed'
				continue
			end 'removed'
			kept.push(opIdx)
		end 'eachRef'
		block.opRefs = kept
	end 'eachBlock'
end 'removeDoomedOps'
